Make Chromium's long-press menu fit a phone.

It is the desktop menu, which is what it looks like: inspect, view source,
save the page, print, cast, translate, a second window and a split view, none
of which a phone has anywhere to put. They go.

A remove-list rather than a keep-list, deliberately. The same menu carries
cut, copy, paste, select all, spelling and the input-method items, and a
keep-list would have had to name every one of them or quietly break every
text field on the phone.

"Open link in new tab" now puts the page in the reader's group, the same as a
link that opens one itself: the group is started around the page they are on
if it has none, the new page goes to its end, and the reader stays where they
are. The shell is told, so it can mark the group's button rather than leave
the new page to be found by accident.

Two things this does not do yet and the shell should know. The item is still
labelled "open in new tab" -- renaming it needs a new string in the resource
bundle, which would read English in every locale until the translations
arrive, and that seemed worse than the old wording. And the rows are still
desktop height: that is MenuConfig, not this menu, and it is worth doing only
if the product keeps Chromium's menu rather than going back to the shell's.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch and
ohos-phone-tab-groups.patch.

